					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tygently" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2038009); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2038009&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608608371" alt="TyGently" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2038009)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tygently" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2038009); return false;" >TyGently</a></span> <span class="odate time_1455900254 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Feb 2016 16:44</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2459750">
									

<p>I'm going to agree with what psul said, and add this: this explanation is basically the least satisfying explanation that could be offered, even more unsatisfying, I'd argue, than never answering the question at all.</p>
<p>I wasn't a big fan of the original video game articles, but I at least thought that there were intriguing questions to be had from them. Why would a modern gaming company emulate an Assyrian god? Why does it resemble bootleg software? Is there a better version out there?</p>
<p>The answer to every question, this article shows, is &quot;haha lol no, an alien did it because he's stupid&quot;. Any hint of intriguing &quot;why&quot; the previous articles evoked is just answered with &quot;because he's bad at making video games, lol&quot;.</p>
<p>I will grant this article a few things: the description of the entity is rather nice, and the foundation pleading for it to continue making video games is somewhat cute. But beyond that, this article obliterates the only things, in my view, the previous articles had going for them, and psul's comment sadly made me realize how formulaic this all is. There isn't a deeper story because the formula barely allows for a basic story.</p>
<p>So, all in all: downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
